{% from 'networkpolicy.yml.j2' import networkpolicy with context %}
---
# Slice 2 of the ingress default-deny.
#
# sonarr and radarr reach this pod as the download client at `wireguard:8080` — a compat Service
# (service-wireguard.yaml.j2) that selects `app: qbittorrent`, carrying the hostname their SQLite
# databases were seeded with under Docker. The Service name differs; the pod is the same, so a
# podSelector on `app: qbittorrent` covers it. The PORT is the WebUI port, which the macro call
# below passes explicitly; it equals the entry's `port` today, and test_netpol_from.py fails if
# the two ever diverge.
#
# This rule is PORT-SCOPED, and for the callers below that scoping is the whole grant: the baseline
# admits none of sonarr, radarr, qbittorrent-reach-probe or homepage, so they reach this pod only through
# this rule and only on the WebUI port.
#
# TRAEFIK IS LISTED HERE DELIBERATELY — do not remove it as redundant — but be accurate about why.
# The baseline admits traefik to every baseline-labelled pod on ANY port, and policies are
# additive, so while qbittorrent carries that label the union is the same with or without this
# entry. It was load-bearing for the deploy that INTRODUCED the label: the old pod carried
# `app: qbittorrent` without `netpol-baseline: enforced`, so this policy was the only one selecting
# it and the UI would have 502'd for the length of that rollout (the "rollout hazard" section of
# the slice-2 plan). That window is closed — every pod created from here on carries both labels.
# It stays because this policy then states its own complete caller set rather than depending on the
# baseline continuing to admit traefik on all ports: if that baseline rule is ever port-scoped, or
# this pod template loses the label, this line is what keeps the UI up.
#
# qbittorrent-reach-probe (tasks/verify.yml, "Reach the WebUI from a pod, as Traefik does") is
# load-bearing here, not incidental: it exists to prove qbittorrent is reachable from the POD
# CIDR specifically, despite the Mullvad kill-switch's iptables rules on the wireguard sidecar —
# the one source address that can detect the kill-switch having eaten the cluster's return path
# (D11 trap). Fence that probe out and the check would still pass-or-fail on this policy alone,
# silently inverting what it asserts: a REACHED would mean nothing, and the one real regression
# this test exists to catch (the kill-switch swallowing pod-CIDR traffic) would read as green.
# Same shape as roles/k8s/registry/templates/networkpolicy.yaml.j2 admitting its own
# registry-selftest-push/registry-selftest-pull Jobs. The probe pod carries this app label only
# because tasks/verify.yml's `kubectl run` sets it with `--labels` — kubectl's default `run:`
# label would not match a podSelector on `app`.
#
# homepage's dashboard widget authenticates to the WebUI over the ClusterIP
# (roles/k8s/homepage/templates/config/services.yaml.j2). It goes direct rather than through Traefik
# because qbittorrent's public route is Authelia-gated — so this entry is the whole grant, and
# fencing it out breaks the widget.
#
# The caller list is `netpol_from:` on this role's containers_list entry
# (inventory/host_vars/daniel-box.yml), each caller's reason beside it. The entry's
# `netpol_role_owned: true` keeps netpol-baseline from rendering a second copy.
{{ networkpolicy(container_item.name, qbittorrent_k8s_webui_port, container_item.netpol_from) }}
